    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ab78658f_34de9dac",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1101621
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-11T00:08:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For crosvm + upstream QEMU, using `android::snaphot::ITextureLoaderPtr` in conjunction with the snapshot apis would be a bit difficult.  Reason being for crosvm, we in theory would have to generate Rust bindings for this, and C++ Rust bindings are trickier than plain old C.  Similarly, QEMU is plain old C and not sure how that would work.\n\nPlus, having an external API for every type of object (buffer, texture) wouldn\u0027t really scale.  But it makes sense an gfxstream internal.\n\nWhat about something like this?\n\n```\nuint8_t *data;\nuint64_t data_size;\nint32_t result \u003d stream_renderer_snapshot(rutabaga, \u0026data, \u0026data_size);\n```\n\nThis is similar to what we discussed in crrev.com/c/4853266 with the CF people interested in snapshot.  \n\nUsing `android::snapshot::ITextureLoaderPtr` internally for gfxstream is still fine btw.  You would just case your `(uint8_t*)` to that.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 527,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "b79b81d727e0c135608573f20c2d8c53e74d83cb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a55667f7_fc2d626b",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1087303
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-11T18:03:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I could change the API to an opaque pointer and cast it to an internal struct in the implementation. I would avoid the `(uint8_t** data, uint64_t* size)` since it implies stream_renderer_snapshot is allocating the output buffer and writing a large amount of data into it (which could go up to a few hundred or even thousands of MB).\n\nWhat would be the use case for crosvm BTW? Do they care about GL snaphsot? Or do they care about VK only?",
      "parentUuid": "ab78658f_34de9dac",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 527,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "b79b81d727e0c135608573f20c2d8c53e74d83cb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "caabdd60_040c6792",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1101621
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-12T17:24:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e allocating the output buffer and writing a large amount of data into it\n\nGood point, I assume for upstream QEMU we are going to end writing to a QEMUFile anyways (crosvm has an equivalent too).\n\nhttps://github.com/qemu/qemu/blob/master/migration/qemu-file.c\n\ncrrev.com/c/4853266 suggested the use of callbacks, so maybe we can use that strategy?\n\n```\n/**\n * # Safety\n * - `data` is valid only for the duration of the call.\n * - Throwing an exception inside this callback is not allowed.\n */\ntypedef void (*stream_renderer_snapshot_write_callback)(uint64_t user_data, const uint8_t* data, size_t len);\n```\nSimilarly for reading.  That more or less matches `SnapshotSaveStream` does: reads or writes POD.\n\n\u003e What would be the use case for crosvm BTW? Do they care about GL snaphsot? Or do they care about VK only?\n\nVK only is the use case for CF.  Right now, snapshots are available with `-gpu_mode\u003dguest_swiftshader` and it actually saves a non-trivial amount of server time.  If we ever want to transition the AOSP CI to `--gpu_mode\u003dgfxstream_guest_angle_host_swiftshader`, the lack of snapshot support may be a regression/blocker:\n\ngo/cloud-android-gpu#x86-64-host-machines",
      "parentUuid": "a55667f7_fc2d626b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 527,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "b79b81d727e0c135608573f20c2d8c53e74d83cb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "15b2772d_13421304",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1087303
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-12T21:04:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What about this, we create 2 APIs, one for crosvm with the write_callback, and the other with more complex parameters for goldfish. Internally it wraps up both APIs into a SnapshotSaver object and we use the SnapshotSaver object. The implementation of aemu::base::Stream seems pretty straight forward and we could create a CallbackStream(write_callback) easily.\n\nThis would give crosvm a clean API, and a flexible API for AEMU to experiment with and optimize. AEMU would need it because our long term plan is to turn most of the snapshot bits into protobuf, and might have an extra file to hold large data buffers.",
      "parentUuid": "caabdd60_040c6792",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 527,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "b79b81d727e0c135608573f20c2d8c53e74d83cb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "773d3f1d_004f0267",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1101621
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-13T00:16:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using `protobuf` and extra files does make sense, even for crosvm.\n\nRegardless of crosvm/AEMU/QEMU, I think we\u0027ll need structured data anyways.  Protobuf is a good solution for that.  For example, we\u0027ll need to describe virtio-gpu resources, and that serialization format could be different from the snapshot decoder serialization format.\n\nFor example, `gfxstream.snapshot` could look like:\n\n```\n\u003cvirtio-gpu resource\u003e\n   \u003cresource_id\u003e 1 \u003c/resource_id\u003e\n   \u003ctype\u003e blob \u003c/type\u003e\n   \u003csize\u003e 4096 \u003c/size\u003e\n\u003c/virtio-gpu resource\u003e \n\u003cvirtio-gpu resource\u003e\n   \u003cresource_id\u003e 2 \u003c/resource_id\u003e\n   \u003ctype\u003e pipe \u003c/type\u003e\n   \u003cwidth\u003e 1024 \u003c/height\u003e\n   \u003cheight\u003e 512 \u003c/height\u003e\n   \u003cformat\u003e NV12 \u003c/format\u003e\n\u003c/virtio-gpu resource\u003e\n\u003cVK Decoder data path\u003e\n   \u003c/usr/local/emulator_snapshots/VkSnapshotDecoder1\u003e\n\u003c/VK Decoder data path\u003e\n\u003cTexture data path\u003e\n   \u003c/usr/local/emulator_snapshots/Texture1\u003e   \n\u003cTexture data path\u003e\n```\n\nThen, the crosvm API would just finding the path the to snapshot file(s), just as `STREAM_RENDERER_PARAM_SNAPSHOT_DATA_DIRECTORY`, which could be `/usr/local/aemu/emulator_snaphots`.  That would also make `android::snapshot::ITextureLoaderPtr` internal to gfxstream, since IIUC it only needs a path to be constructed.  WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "15b2772d_13421304",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 527,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "b79b81d727e0c135608573f20c2d8c53e74d83cb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "93eed2c4_afb6751c",
        "filename": "common/end2end/GfxstreamEnd2EndTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1087303
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-16T23:30:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is great to hear that crosvm would like to take the protobuf + extra large data files approach as well. I am not going to ask crosvm to pass ITextureLoaderPtr as input parameters so don\u0027t worry about it.\n\nNote that we are not going to know the snapshot path during initialization or in general anytime before a snapshot command is issued. This is because users are free to create multiple snapshots with different names (thus different paths). We need to pass them by parameters as strings, pointers to files, or write_bytes callback functions (might need seekp / tellp as well).\n\nFor now I am just going to get something up and running real quick. We will rework the API later when we rewrite VkDecoderSnapshot into protobufs. At that point we should also understand exactly what snapshot files we will write to.",
      "parentUuid": "773d3f1d_004f0267",
